My old neighbor, Wilson, had lived in South America for many years before he came back to England.
He had always wished to spend his last years in England and had decided to live in a small village.
He had no sooner arrived than he rented a lovely cottage and moved into it.
Almost at once he began to grumble about the climate, for although it was still summer, it rained every day and the wind was very cold.
After so many years of warmth, Wilson was quite surprised.
He behaved as if he had never known England before.
In the end, it was too much for him.
He had scarcely unpacked his things when he gave up the cottage and went abroad.
The dream he had kept for such a long time was broken there.
Wilson had prepared for everything except the climate.